**Stale ConfigMaps accumulate across configuration changes**

Every time the data map changes, a new ConfigMap with a different hash-suffixed name is created, but the old ConfigMap (e.g. `keystone-config-a1b2c3d4`) is **never deleted**. The owner reference only triggers GC when the owner object itself is deleted — it does not clean up superseded siblings with stale hashes.

In a typical OpenStack service lifecycle, configuration data changes regularly (e.g. new endpoint URLs, policy updates, feature flags). Each change permanently orphans the prior ConfigMap. On a long-lived cluster, hundreds of stale immutable ConfigMaps can accumulate in a namespace, consuming etcd storage.

The caller (reconciler) knows the previous ConfigMap name (it was returned on the prior reconcile) and could delete it after confirming the new one is adopted. A cleaner option is to add a label such as `forge.c5c3.io/config-hash-for: <owner-name>` to every ConfigMap created here and have `CreateImmutableConfigMap` list + delete all ConfigMaps owned by `owner` whose hash suffix no longer matches the current hash before returning. Example sketch:

```go
// After CreateOrUpdate succeeds, clean up stale versions.
var cmList corev1.ConfigMapList
if err := c.List(ctx, &cmList,
    client.InNamespace(owner.GetNamespace()),
    client.MatchingLabels{labelOwner: owner.GetName(), labelManaged: "true"},
); err == nil {
    for _, old := range cmList.Items {
        if old.Name != hashedName {
            _ = c.Delete(ctx, &old)
        }
    }
}
```

Without cleanup, `CreateImmutableConfigMap` has an unbounded memory/storage leak for any owner that is long-lived.

